  "Area": "Unclassified",
  "Number": 6,
  "Question": "The sums of three whole numbers taken in pairs are \\(12,\\) \\(17,\\) and \\(19.\\) What is the middle number?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(4\\)",
    "B": "\\(5\\)",
    "C": "\\(6\\)",
    "D": "\\(7\\)",
    "E": "\\(8\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Adding the three pair sums counts each original number exactly twice, because each number is paired with each of the other two. Thus, if \\(S\\) is the sum of the three numbers,\n\\[\nS=\\frac{12+17+19}{2}=24.\n\\]\nEach pair sum leaves out one number. Subtracting each pair sum from \\(S\\) therefore recovers all three numbers:\n\\[\n24-19=5,\\qquad 24-17=7,\\qquad 24-12=12.\n\\]\nThese are whole numbers with the required pair sums: \\(5+7=12\\), \\(5+12=17\\), and \\(7+12=19\\). Since \\(5<7<12\\), the middle number is \\(\\boxed{7}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
